---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoConverter from '@/components/tools/media/VideoConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="video-converter">
  <VideoConverter client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a video file</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your video onto the box above, or click it to browse. MP4, MOV, MKV, WebM, AVI, MPEG and 3GP all work.</li>
      <li>Choose an output format: <strong>MP4 (H.264)</strong> for something that plays everywhere, <strong>WebM (VP9)</strong> for a smaller file, or <strong>GIF</strong> for a silent animation.</li>
      <li>Pick a quality target and, for a smaller file, a lower resolution. The tool only scales videos down, never up.</li>
      <li>Press convert. The first run downloads the FFmpeg engine once (10 MB); after that it is cached and starts at once.</li>
      <li>Preview the result in the page and download it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Which video format and codec to choose</h2>
    <p>
      A video file has two parts: the container (MP4, MKV, WebM), which holds the streams, and the codec (H.264,
      VP9, AV1), which compresses the pictures. Most compatibility problems come from the codec, not the
      container.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Output</th>
            <th>Codecs</th>
            <th>Plays on</th>
            <th>Encode speed</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>MP4</td>
            <td>H.264 + AAC</td>
            <td>Everything since about 2008</td>
            <td>Fast</td>
            <td>Sharing, editing, uploading anywhere</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>WebM</td>
            <td>VP9 + Opus</td>
            <td>All modern browsers; poor native support on older TVs and iOS apps</td>
            <td>5-10× slower</td>
            <td>Web pages where bandwidth matters</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>GIF</td>
            <td>256-colour palette, no audio</td>
            <td>Everything, including email</td>
            <td>Fast</td>
            <td>Short silent loops, documentation, chat</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What the quality setting does</h2>
    <p>
      Here both H.264 and VP9 use CRF (constant rate factor). CRF fixes the perceived quality and lets the
      bitrate change: a static interview scene uses few bits, a handheld action shot uses many. So the output
      size cannot be predicted exactly before encoding.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Preset</th>
            <th>H.264 CRF</th>
            <th>VP9 CRF</th>
            <th>Result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>High quality</td>
            <td>18</td>
            <td>26</td>
            <td>Indistinguishable from the source on close inspection</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Balanced</td>
            <td>23</td>
            <td>33</td>
            <td>The usual choice: small file, no obvious artefacts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Small file</td>
            <td>28</td>
            <td>40</td>
            <td>Visible softening in detailed or fast-moving shots</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why convert video in the browser</h2>
    <p>
      This page runs FFmpeg, compiled to WebAssembly, inside your own tab. Your laptop is slower than a rented
      encoding server, so long videos take longer. In exchange, the footage stays on your device, the only size
      limit is your own memory, and the tool works on a plane.
    </p>

    <h2>Practical limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Memory: WebAssembly in a browser is limited to about 2 GB, and it holds both the input and the output. Files up to about 500 MB work well.</li>
      <li>Speed: the tool uses the single-threaded FFmpeg core, which needs no special server headers. Expect about 0.3-1× real time for H.264 on a modern laptop.</li>
      <li>Hardware encoders: browsers do not give WebAssembly access to them, so encoding uses only the CPU. A faster preset is the main way to save time.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
